issue_texts.718:
Explicit string tags on `<<` keys are ignored when merging is enabled

### Checklist

- [x] I have read the [Contributing Guidelines](https://github.com/eemeli/yaml/blob/main/docs/CONTRIBUTING.md).
- [x] I affirm that I am submitting my own writing, and not the output of an LLM or similar tool.

### Describe the bug

With `merge: true`, a plain `<<` key is treated as a merge key even when it has an explicit `!!str` or non-specific `!` tag.

### To Reproduce

```js
import { parseDocument } from 'yaml'

const options = { merge: true }

console.log(parseDocument('!!str <<: { x: 1 }', options).toJS())
// Actual: { x: 1 }
// Expected: { '<<': { x: 1 } }

console.log(parseDocument('! <<: { x: 1 }', options).toJS())
// Actual: { x: 1 }
// Expected: { '<<': { x: 1 } }

console.log(parseDocument('"<<": { x: 1 }', options).toJS())
// Correct: { '<<': { x: 1 } }
```

A scalar value also triggers an unexpected error:

```js
parseDocument('!!str <<: 5', options).toJS()
// Throws: Merge sources must be maps or map aliases
// Expected: { '<<': 5 }
```

### Expected behaviour

See `Expected` comments above

### `yaml` library version

2.9.1

### Environment

Node.js 22.23.2

### Additional context

I think the issue is that the parsed scalar retains its explicit tag, but the fallback `isMergeKey()` check doesn't inspect that tag. Happy to make a PR if you want.

